{"qid":"Q400998","names":["Ahmadnagar Sultanate"],"title":"Ahmadnagar Sultanate","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Ahmadnagar Sultanate was one of the five Deccan Sultanates, founded in 1490 when Malik Ahmad Nizam Shah declared independence from the disintegrating Bahmani Sultanate and established his capital at the newly built city of Ahmadnagar. At its height in the mid-16th century the sultanate was a significant military power: it participated in the confederate victory over the Vijayanagara Empire at the Battle of Talikota (1565) and resisted Mughal expansion for decades under the regent Chand Bibi, who defended Ahmadnagar against Murad and Abu'l-Fath Khan's sieges in 1595–1596. The Mughals under Akbar and Jahangir progressively dismembered the sultanate; Malik Ambar, the Ethiopian-born minister and general, led an effective resistance movement from 1607 to 1626. The last sultan, Husain Nizam Shah III, was captured and the sultanate formally annexed by Shah Jahan in 1636.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Core territory is reasonably well attested through Mughal and Deccan chronicles; the outer marches toward Berar and the Konkan coast fluctuated with military campaigns and are approximately rather than precisely mapped.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Ahmadnagar Sultanate","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ahmadnagar_Sultanate"},{"label":"Britannica: Ahmadnagar Sultanate","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Ahmadnagar-Sultanate"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Ahmadnagar Sultanate","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Ahmad Nizam Shah I","nativeName":"احمد نظام شاه اول","